 "fit": {
  "intro": "Each tool here solves the same 13-stage adaptive problem to the same tolerance and agrees with the analytic orbit; they differ in how the per-sample control flow is written and run. Numbers below are at N = 1,000,000; the fastest arm there is eagle.simulate (eagle picks the launch mode) (2.32 s).",
  "arms": {
   "eagle_graph_persist": "the plain (not active-set) attempt kernel, forced through hawk's persist entry: one launch, no graph, no map, no policy kernel -- a grid of SMs blocks of 256 lanes steals the next unfinished sample off a counter when their own finishes: 2.32 s; the eagle graph takes 1.69\u00d7 as long.",
   "eagle_simulate": "the same kernel through eagle.simulate, its state and parameters by name (21 lines with the kernel): 2.32 s; the eagle graph takes 1.69\u00d7 as long.",
   "eagle_graph": "the attempt is per-sample scalar code (22 lines with its launch; the kernel finishes its own samples), one kernel per attempt looped on the device: 3.91 s.",
   "warp_kernel": "an explicit per-thread loop in a Python-embedded kernel language (74 lines, its own stages): 4.26 s.",
   "jax_vmap": "writes the per-sample loop directly (25 lines) and compiles the whole batch into one executable: 7.24 s.",
   "torch_compiled": "compiles a block of 16 masked attempts with torch.compile (reduce-overhead): \u2013; eager PyTorch takes \u2013 as long.",
   "cupy_masked": "array code in the NumPy style (25 lines), the control flow as masks over the batch: 52.8 s.",
   "torch_graphed": "records 16 attempts as a CUDA graph and replays them, removing per-kernel launch cost: 55.3 s; eager PyTorch takes \u2013 as long."
  }
 },
